1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now it's to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
So a couple of weeks ago, a friend of mine invited me to go cross-country skiing.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Never done it before. I thought, hey, why not? Give it a shot.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Well, here it is two weeks later.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
I'm like, you know, nearly 50 miles away from home.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
I am tired.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
I think we probably should have tried, you know, a smaller country for our first time out, don't you?

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Hey, this is Podcast Number 58.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast. This is Clark Green.

10
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Hey, let's take a look at the emails that came in in the past week or two.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Frank Vaynard from Novi, Michigan, where he works with Troop 407 and Pack 54 had this to say.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
He said, first, thanks for the shout out on Podcast 57. That's last week's podcast.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
I appreciate the nice thing you said about Bob White's blather.

14
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
That is Frank's blog, and he's a Troop Committee Chairperson.

15
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,000
I believe that the Troop Committee's job is to support the Scoutmaster and the Troop program,

16
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
and I try and write from that perspective.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Well, he does a real good job of writing from that perspective,

18
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
and I think you should go over and check out the blog.

19
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,000
I'll recommend that to you again this week.

20
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,000
There will be a link to the blog on the post that contains this podcast.

21
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
He goes on to say, I've been catching up on your recent podcasts since I started listening a few weeks ago,

22
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
and it's always nice to hear from a veteran Scouter.

23
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
I also like the way the podcasts are produced. They're smooth, sound good.

24
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:16,000
And, okay, hang on.

25
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,000
All right, we try and keep them smooth and sound good as we can, Frank.

26
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Having worked in broadcasting for over 35 years,

27
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
I can tell when someone has a sense for what an audio program should sound like.

28
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
I was intrigued by the Scouter who is anticipating a tidal wave of weeblos,

29
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,000
because that's what our troop went through about four years ago.

30
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Any troop facing an influx of membership needs to remember to stick with the patrol method,

31
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
prepare the troop's youth leaders, make new Scouts and families welcome,

32
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
and keep an eye on the fundamentals.

33
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Then they'll be on their way to an active, high-functioning troop.

34
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
When we did this, we made a few mistakes,

35
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
and some folks resisted allowing the boys to actually be in charge,

36
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
but as we grew, it became apparent that the patrol method is really the only way to go.

37
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Thanks for the wealth of information delivered in an entertaining and listenable manner.

38
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
I look forward to your future podcasts and hope to contribute and participate.

39
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Well, thank you so much, Frank.

40
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
It sure does make it a little bit easier when you get nice, encouraging emails like that.

41
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
I really appreciate it.

42
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Mike Malone wrote in and said this,

43
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Keep up the good work. I've been a journalist and author for 30 years,

44
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
so I know how hard it is to keep writing year after year.

45
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Yeah, tell me about it, Mike.

46
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
I'm a huge admirer of your work, and I hope you stay with it for years to come.

47
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
It is really having an impact out there.

48
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Wow, two really good paychecks in two really good emails.

49
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
I sure do appreciate it.

50
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Jamie Humphries from PAC 685 in Pflugerville, Texas wrote in to say this,

51
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,000
I've been listening to all the archives as I sit on many a plane flight these past couple of weeks.

52
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
They're really enlightening.

53
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
I'm new to Scouting as an adult leader. I wasn't a Scout growing up,

54
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
but I grew up in the woods of northeast Texas,

55
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and we just kind of naturally did a lot of the things that Scouts do.

56
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
When my kids couldn't have those fantastic experiences I had growing up,

57
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
we joined Scouts.

58
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
My wife calls it the BSA Mafia.

59
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Because once you get in, the deeper you go.

60
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
I started out running the cars at a Pinewood Derby.

61
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
We all know what happens then, right?

62
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Then I went on to become a Weeblows Den Leader. Yep.

63
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And then a Chaplain. And then an Assistant Cubmaster.

64
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
And now I'm about to become Cubmaster.

65
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
And I'm a Unit Commissioner all at the same time.

66
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Now my daughter is joining a Venture Crew, so my closet is getting full of uniforms.

67
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Jason, it sounds like a lot of fun.

68
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
It sounds like a lot of fun. Man, a lot of us have been there too.

69
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
And thanks for getting in touch.

70
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Larry Geiger had this to say about my review of Les Stroud's Will to Live.

71
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,000
In part of the book, Les says, yet beneath it all our hidden desire,

72
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
our secret guilty pleasure, is to one day put our skills to the test

73
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
when life hangs in the balance.

74
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And Larry says, no, not me.

75
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
I really, really like coming back safe.

76
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,000
I agree with you, Larry.

77
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Even more, he goes on to say, I like everyone else to come back safe.

78
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
I think that while we do spend a good amount of time in the outdoors,

79
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
our scouts aren't in the kinds of situations that require survival skills like Les describes.

80
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,000
However, some of our scouts are heavily at risk in college and young adulthood.

81
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
They may take on challenges that they are really not prepared for.

82
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Scouts have told me stories of some things they did after scouting

83
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
and how they survived out there in the wilderness.

84
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,000
I think it's good to sit around the campfire and chat about these things from time to time

85
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
and tell the scouts what can happen.

86
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,000
You know, I might even buy this book.

87
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Well, Larry, you know, I hadn't really thought of it that way, but you are right.

88
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,000
You know, you go out camping with the scouts and everything goes great when you're in high school.

89
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
And then you get into college and into young adulthood and you say, hey, what could go wrong?

90
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
We never had any problem when we were in scouts.

91
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Well, yeah, I think you've got it right, Larry.

92
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
I've had some stories come back to me about boys who, you know,

93
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,000
graduated from high school, went on to college, decided they would go out, go out hiking and stuff like that.

94
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
He got themselves in a bit of a pickle.

95
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
So that's a real good point, Larry.

96
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
We might want to sit around the campfire and talk about what can happen.

97
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
I think I will begin to do that.

98
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
So this week we have an interview with author Les Stroud.

99
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
And I was really excited to be able to talk to him.

100
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Les Stroud is probably best known to most of us for his work in the Survivor Man series.

101
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
We'll talk a little bit about that during the interview.

102
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And then we have a phone call that came in about a favorite camping trip.

103
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
So that's an awful lot to get done today.

104
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Let's get started, shall we?

105
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Let's get started.

106
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Today I'm speaking with Survivor Man Les Stroud about his book Will to Live.

107
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Welcome to the Scoutmaster Podcast, Les.

108
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:55,000
How are you?

109
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,000
I'm doing real well.

110
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
How are things up there in Huntsville?

111
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Very well. Very well. Good.

112
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
You grew up in Toronto, if I have it right.

113
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
That's correct. Yeah, just to the west end of Toronto, Mimico.

114
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Did you do any scouting when you were growing up?

115
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Just a very little bit when I was younger, yes, I did.

116
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
There was an active scout troop in the area.

117
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
I think at some point it might have dissolved at some point.

118
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
But yes, when I was young, I was out for sure.

119
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,000
It was great.

120
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And my sister also did Girl Guides.

121
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:32,000
So then you ended up somewhere along the line in Tomogamie in a canoe.

122
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:42,000
For many years, I grew up as a big fan of the wilderness and nature and all of that stuff,

123
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
whatever the TV would offer me.

124
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
But I would go to my cottage and definitely I would make little shelters

125
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:54,000
and I would get out in the back 40 and sort of just play around

126
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
and do everything I had to out in the back.

127
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
I just loved it, adored it.

128
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
But I also got away from it as I got a little bit older and got into music.

129
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,000
And then I got back into it again when I discovered canoe tripping up in Tomogamie.

130
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
It's a fantastic area.

131
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
It's like the jewel of the north.

132
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
I love Algonquin Park, but Tomogamie is kind of like Algonquin Park with no people.

133
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:14,000
So it's beautiful.

134
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
We bring a crew of boys up and do a week in Algonquin every summer.

135
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Yes, it's beautiful there.

136
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
So your trip to Tomogamie, that was kind of a turning point

137
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
and you ended up becoming a canoe guide.

138
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Yes, it was after many years of being a musician and working in film

139
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
and all of that sort of stuff that I finally got invited at some point

140
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
on a canoe trip with some friends.

141
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
And I just absolutely loved it.

142
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,000
It was so fantastic and so beautiful in Tomogamie to do it.

143
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:46,000
And once I got that taste of that sort of outdoor adventure life for real,

144
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
I know, bear in mind, I was older now.

145
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
I never looked back.

146
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:55,000
Yes, and that led to you setting up a production company

147
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:01,000
that put together a couple of survivor specials for the Canadian Discovery Channel.

148
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:06,000
Yes, what it was was I always wanted to do a home DVD series,

149
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,000
which actually back then would have been a home VHS and video series on survival.

150
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:17,000
But at one point I realized that the time was right for me to pitch it to the television networks.

151
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
And so I did that instead of just producing it on my own.

152
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
And lo and behold, they loved the idea.

153
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
So the very first Survivorman sort of thing that I did was,

154
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:31,000
since the two pilot versions, I call them Stranded,

155
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:37,000
was for Discovery Channel Canada at the time.

156
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And I just loved doing it, and it went over so, so well

157
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
that I then took it upon myself to pitch it as a full-on series called Survivorman.

158
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
That's where most of the people who are listening are going to know you from,

159
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
from that series. It's just a great series, one of my favorites.

160
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Thank you. Yes. Well, it's a, I mean, and it's, you know, people,

161
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
I don't think people realize that I did it for, for nearly nine years,

162
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
and then they moved on to do Beyond Survival, which is the next series,

163
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and is out playing, it's playing right now constantly,

164
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
which was like Survivorman all grown up because it was out surviving

165
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
with the indigenous peoples around the world.

166
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
So it's been, it's been over 10 years that I've been doing it.

167
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Yeah. The one thing I really love about this show is how honest it is

168
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:23,000
and how, you know, nothing is really romanticized.

169
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
The thing I think about are the scenes where you're up at night

170
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
in a shelter somewhere with that kind of night vision green lighting,

171
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
and you're talking about, you know, being afraid

172
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
or being kind of uncertain about what's going to happen next.

173
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
That just, I've actually been in a situation like that,

174
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,000
and I don't know how you can do a better job of explaining

175
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
what the whole thing feels like.

176
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
Well, I think, you know, that was the whole deal,

177
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
was to, you know, just to really truly go through stuff.

178
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
I mean, I obviously did hundreds of survival experiences

179
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:03,000
before I started filming them, and so this was a matter

180
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,000
of just going through the way I always let the real things happen,

181
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
let the chips fall as they may, pull no punches,

182
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
fumble and bumble like anybody else would,

183
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
have some successes like anybody else would,

184
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and then as I film it, you know, try to capture it

185
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
in a way that is captivating, but at the same time,

186
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
just tell the story as it is,

187
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
and don't try to trump up the drama.

188
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Just show it as it is, because I think

189
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
being lost in the wilderness is drama enough.

190
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
And it's not fun. I mean, it's not...

191
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
No, it's never fun. I think that's the big thing, too,

192
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
is people, you know, it's become

193
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
a recreational activity survival has.

194
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:42,000
It's become that, and partially through shows like my own,

195
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
but survival in its true essence

196
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
is nothing recreational about it.

197
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
It's miserable. It's uncomfortable. It's cold.

198
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
And in reality, you don't want to build a shelter.

199
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
You don't want to build traps.

200
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
You don't want to make this or that.

201
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
You don't want to be, you know,

202
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
MacGyvering items into other items.

203
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
What you want is to go home.

204
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
That's the key to survival, is getting out and going home.

205
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
That is the whole point of survival, is going home.

206
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Once it becomes a sort of recreational side of things,

207
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
well, that's fun because the skills are really enjoyable,

208
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
and that's how you hone your survival abilities.

209
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
But in truth, you really just want to go home.

210
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
And that kind of puts us right into the book, Will to Live.

211
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
I've read a number of books about survival and things like that,

212
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
but I think this is really unique,

213
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
because it's kind of like three books in one.

214
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
You have the narrative,

215
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
and then you have the analysis of what goes on,

216
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
and then you have a lot of practical advice kind of woven in.

217
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:41,000
Yeah, well, I mean, I wanted to keep the book very alive and flowing.

218
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,000
And I also know that there have been a number of books out there

219
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
that have discussed different survival stories,

220
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
but they always come from the angle of the psychology.

221
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,000
It's like, oh, the psychology, the psychology, the will to live, the mental.

222
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,000
And I'm like saying, well, yeah, okay, I get it.

223
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
But what about if survival is 90% in the mind,

224
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
what about that last 10%?

225
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Because that last 10% is the stuff that, you know,

226
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
is how do you get from A to B,

227
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
and what are the right decisions to make,

228
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
and what do you eat and don't eat and all?

229
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
And those things can be ridiculously fascinating.

230
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
And it was my chance to take a look at some of these stories,

231
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
and then after my years of experience of doing it,

232
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,000
also inject my opinion and say, well, you know, that was a dumb move.

233
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
You know, this person should never have done that,

234
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
no matter what way you slice it. I get it.

235
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
And I wasn't there, but generally speaking, that was a dumb move.

236
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:34,000
Or, you know what? I wasn't there. None of us were there.

237
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
This would not seem like a good move,

238
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,000
but it's pretty hard to judge because we were not there.

239
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:43,000
And then conversely, finally, would be that was a brilliant move.

240
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
You know, I have no idea how he had the gumption to get up and do that,

241
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,000
but, you know, thinking to do that certain thing saved his life.

242
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
And the stories run the gamut of that and many other things.

243
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
That's what I think is really the strength of the book.

244
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:00,000
You get a really good picture of what you call a survivor's mindset.

245
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
What is that mindset?

246
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:10,000
Well, the bottom line is remembering that survival is,

247
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
for all intents and purposes, it's a verb.

248
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
You know, it's proactive.

249
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
There is nothing passive about survival,

250
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
and that's something that I always try to get across.

251
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:24,000
You cannot be passive. You cannot be mild and meek and gentle

252
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
and flow with the energy of the force.

253
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,000
No, you're starving and you have to go home

254
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
and you have to scale a wall to do it.

255
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:36,000
Survival is a practical, sometimes aggressive activity,

256
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and you want to be active in your own survival.

257
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:43,000
Because if you're going to be passive and let things come what may,

258
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:44,000
then guess what you're going to get?

259
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,000
You're going to get things come what may, and they could be bad things.

260
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:55,000
So that mindset is the mindset of being strong in your mind to know.

261
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:59,000
You know, it's the same mindset as the person who gets up off the couch.

262
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
You know, those who sit on the couch and don't get up,

263
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
and there are those of us who get up off the couch.

264
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
And that's what life is about.

265
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
Life is about getting up off the couch and doing.

266
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:15,000
And survival is perhaps an extreme example of that,

267
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,000
where if you don't get up and do something, you very well may die.

268
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:26,000
So the mindset is one for survival that is very proactive and self-preservative.

269
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:32,000
You talk about additive factors in survival,

270
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:36,000
and you kind of analyze those factors like the will to live, like luck,

271
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and things like that.

272
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:42,000
Well, I think I was really glad in Will to Live to take the end of each chapter.

273
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,000
And it's a bit of a device, it's a bit of a literary device for me to say,

274
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
okay, I'm going to actually grade these guys.

275
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:54,000
And it's a bit of a guess, but looking at their stories,

276
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
you can look at something like the Robertsons in their life raft floating in the ocean.

277
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Well, for goodness sakes, they had turtles bump into their raft nearly every single day,

278
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
providing them with ample food, and rainstorms every time they needed it,

279
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
providing them with ample water.

280
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
And that's that additive force that we call luck.

281
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
I was like, wow, that's pretty lucky.

282
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,000
And then Yossi Ginsburg, in terms of Will to Live,

283
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:20,000
I mean, everything happened to this guy in the jungle.

284
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
I mean, just when you couldn't think it could get worse, it would.

285
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:27,000
And yet he kept on surviving, and his will was absolute.

286
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,000
So his luck was terrible, but his will to keep going was supreme.

287
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:40,000
So there are so many variables in the additive forces of what it takes to survive.

288
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
Like what kind of kit do you have with you?

289
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
And what kind of knowledge base do you have?

290
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
The guys up on the top of the mountain in the Andes,

291
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:52,000
the Uruguayan rugby team that had to resort to cannibalism, had no survival skills.

292
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Some of these kids had never even been out of the city.

293
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
So zero survival skills.

294
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Whereas Douglas Lawson, in his trek across the Antarctic,

295
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
was the most supreme Arctic survivor ever.

296
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:08,000
So his skills were supreme, yet both of them found themselves in near-death experiences.

297
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:13,000
So it's really interesting to see how different individuals play out,

298
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:19,000
given their circumstances, and depending on what additive force they're strong in.

299
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:20,000
Do they have a lot of luck?

300
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
Do they have a great survival kit?

301
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Do they have an incredible amount of knowledge?

302
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
Or do they have a supreme will to live?

303
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
Those are the four top ones.

304
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
After that, there's another 20 variables.

305
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:37,000
And I guess the way of guaranteeing or preparing yourself

306
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:43,000
is to try and make sure that all those additive factors are on your side.

307
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
As much as you can, and that's preparation.

308
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Preparation is the key there.

309
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
You can get the knowledge ahead of time.

310
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:53,000
You can have a well-stocked survival kit.

311
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:59,000
You can bolster your will to live even by having this knowledge,

312
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
having the knowledge of where you're going, where this escape is.

313
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:03,000
So what's the escape route?

314
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:08,000
You're going out with a group and you've got a guide.

315
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Well, ask the guide if you can see the map.

316
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,000
Get him to show you where is the closest roads, where is the escape route.

317
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:18,000
So if you get halfway down the river and the guide himself has an appendicitis attack,

318
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:22,000
you might be the only one who knows that there's a highway two miles to the east.

319
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:27,000
And when you prepare yourself and bolster your will to live

320
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,000
by getting that knowledge from the guides themselves,

321
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:33,000
you become an asset, not a liability.

322
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:36,000
And then you don't have to worry about the bad luck so much

323
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,000
because these other three angles you can build up.

324
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:43,000
And that again is being proactive about the situation,

325
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
not just letting things happen to you, but making things happen.

326
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:47,000
Yeah, absolutely.

327
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,000
What do you think is the most important way for people

328
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,000
who are going to take a group of young people into the wilderness

329
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:57,000
to prepare themselves for those eventualities?

330
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:02,000
I think the most important thing is the sharing of the knowledge.

331
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:05,000
We should all know what we all know.

332
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:11,000
You don't want just the leader to know what the escape routes are,

333
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:15,000
where the first aid kit is, what's in the first aid kit,

334
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
how to use it, where the survival kit is.

335
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:21,000
That's the way it happens, especially with younger people too

336
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:24,000
because younger people get out and they don't want to ask.

337
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
Well, they should ask.

338
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:27,000
You're going out into the wilderness.

339
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
You're going out with 12 people.

340
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
You're going to go down a neat river.

341
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:34,000
And even if it's Algonquin Park,

342
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:38,000
anything can happen in the middle of the Petawawa River in Algonquin Park.

343
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
You could find that you're with a group of six

344
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:44,000
and you're the only one that survives a terrible fall down a waterfall.

345
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:47,000
And it happens to be an off time of year,

346
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:51,000
or for whatever reason, nobody's coming down that river for another 12 days.

347
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
You don't know that, but maybe that's the case.

348
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
What do you do next, right?

349
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:58,000
And so I think to answer your question more succinctly,

350
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
what's the best thing?

351
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
It's to share the knowledge with everybody.

352
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:06,000
If you're going on an adventure, you should know as much as your guide does.

353
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:10,000
Is there a particular piece of gear or anything like that

354
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:16,000
that you think is really key to carry into a situation when you're out in the wilderness?

355
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,000
Well, if you're asking for one item, it's tricky.

356
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:28,000
On a level of rescue, I think the new GPS satellite messaging units are pretty great.

357
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:31,000
If it's a matter of personal survival,

358
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:35,000
I think the ability to get a fire going anywhere, anytime is a big deal.

359
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:39,000
So for me, I would like to have three different methods of fire starting.

360
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:43,000
And I'm not talking about the cool, funky flint and striker things.

361
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:46,000
I'm talking about a butane lighter.

362
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:52,000
Everybody wants to be cool about it and talk about strikers and fire bows and fire pistons and this and that.

363
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:53,000
I love those things.

364
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
They're the cool part of the skill set.

365
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
But what do I carry with me?

366
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:00,000
A butane lighter, a pack of matches, and a flint striker.

367
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:01,000
I have all three.

368
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:06,000
Because the last thing I want to be out there is cold and wet.

369
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
And so how do you combat that big fire?

370
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:09,000
That's how you do it.

371
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:14,000
And one thing, and especially since I know in terms of scouting and that,

372
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:18,000
and it's very, and not, I know it all depends, right?

373
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
But when you get the proper training,

374
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:24,000
when you really learn how to handle and manage a fire in the wilderness,

375
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:30,000
then you are better off at that point with a good, solid, robust fire to keep you warm,

376
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:34,000
to dry your clothes, to keep going than a tiny little fire.

377
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:40,000
Because a tiny little fire you have to hover over and maintain and pouring rain will put it out now.

378
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
But remember what my first words were.

379
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:49,000
Once you properly get the skill set on how to maintain and manage a fire in the wilderness,

380
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
which is a skill set all in itself.

381
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:54,000
It's something really to be learned, that's for sure.

382
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:55,000
Yeah.

383
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:58,000
Whoever said wherever there's smoke, there's fire was never a scout master.

384
00:22:58,000 --> 00:22:59,000
That's right.

385
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:02,000
Yeah, it doesn't always, one doesn't always follow the other.

386
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
No, you can't dry your clothes with smoke.

387
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,000
And you know, you do a dump in a set of rapids and you come out soaking wet,

388
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:12,000
you want to dry your clothes and do that, you know.

389
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,000
So for me, and I don't care if I'm in the desert or the jungle or the Arctic,

390
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:17,000
I want to be able to get a fire going.

391
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:20,000
And the Arctic is probably the trickiest place because there's not that much wood up there,

392
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:24,000
but hey, even if it's just a moss fire, I want to be able to get warm and dry.

393
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
So that's my personal item.

394
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:30,000
But again, as I said before, on a larger level, in the rescue level,

395
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:33,000
I think the new GPS satellite messages are pretty good.

396
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:36,000
Yeah, and the sat phones and things like that.

397
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:37,000
Exactly.

398
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
They have kind of changed the game in a lot of ways.

399
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:40,000
They have, definitely.

400
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:44,000
Do you have like a favorite camping story you'd like to share with us?

401
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:47,000
Well, I mean, I have been, I mean, I think you can Google it too,

402
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:52,000
if you Google the best story ever, which was, you know, for me,

403
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:56,000
at the peak of my game, so to speak, when I was instructing a lot

404
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:00,000
and I was feeling very overconfident as a survival instructor

405
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
and doing the group and doing lots of scout groups

406
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:05,000
and because I've trained and taught the scouts lots

407
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,000
and different groups and tourism groups and people,

408
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:12,000
I went off to find a spot and to teach some survival and I came back

409
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:19,000
and I ended up messing around too much with a beautiful cow moose

410
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:23,000
I saw in the swamp and I called out to her and I called out again

411
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:26,000
and I am getting chased through the forest by a 1,500 pound bull moose

412
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:30,000
in the rutting season and I got treed by that bull moose

413
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
while he broke branches and trees down beneath me

414
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:35,000
and I ended up getting out of the tree and running again

415
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:38,000
and he chased me again and I ended up in the lake swimming

416
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:41,000
to get away from a 1,500 pound bull moose in the rutting season.

417
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:45,000
That is, that was, and that, the lesson, the moral of that story,

418
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:49,000
you cannot, just like Aaron, I'd love to meet Aaron Ralston,

419
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:51,000
the man who had to cut his own arm off

420
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:54,000
because his arrogance had him in that position

421
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:57,000
where nobody knew where he was.

422
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:00,000
My arrogance had me in that position with the bull moose

423
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
and nobody knew I was there.

424
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
I had not told anybody where I was going.

425
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:05,000
Stupid, stupid, stupid.

426
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
He just always let people know.

427
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:12,000
So for me, for me, it's funny because I was at the peak of my game

428
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:15,000
and it was when I made the most stupidest blunder

429
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,000
which just goes, you know, you go figure.

430
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:20,000
So you say that people who study survival

431
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:23,000
don't really get a chance to practice the one thing they know best.

432
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:26,000
That's getting caught in a real survival situation,

433
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
like something that's unexpected.

434
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:31,000
Are you telling me you haven't been close enough to that?

435
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
Is that something that you really want to do?

436
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:36,000
I suppose actually that moose story is pretty close to that

437
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:39,000
because that was a bit of, I, when I was running in the bush,

438
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
I almost got completely turned around and lost.

439
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
I've been turned around once or twice.

440
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:46,000
But I guess my point in that is that it's kind of funny

441
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
that those of us, and boys guys included,

442
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,000
and any of us who have that love of the wilderness

443
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,000
and love of adventure, if we also develop a love

444
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
for survival skills and techniques,

445
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:59,000
there comes a point where you just kind of think,

446
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
you know, it'd be kind of cool if I really got lost

447
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:04,000
because then I really put the skills to the test.

448
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
It's like this little guilty secret hidden pleasure.

449
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:09,000
But the reality is that as you get better,

450
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:12,000
the chances that you're really going to get lost

451
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:15,000
just keep getting less and less and less.

452
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:18,000
And unless you're in a situation like me where,

453
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:22,000
as I said, my own arrogance had me treated by a moose,

454
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:25,000
unless you get overly cocky and that happens,

455
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:28,000
the reality is you're probably not going to get lost

456
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
because now you just know too much.

457
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
And, you know, that's a good thing

458
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:36,000
because nobody wants to, we might want to test ourselves,

459
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:39,000
but until it actually happens and then we'd be going,

460
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:41,000
oh, I'm so stupid, I do not want to test,

461
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:42,000
but I just want to go home.

462
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
And that's, again, the moral of the story.

463
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:47,000
And survival, the reality is we just really want to go home.

464
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:50,000
Well, you know, I really appreciate you taking the time with me today.

465
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:53,000
And I was very excited to get the book and to read it.

466
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,000
I recommend it highly.

467
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
I think it's one of the best ones out there.

468
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
Well, thank you very much.

469
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
And you may or may not be aware

470
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:04,000
that I've got my Survivor Man Scout badge out now in Canada.

471
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:05,000
Oh, really?

472
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:09,000
And, yeah, so that's just a thrill.

473
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
I've got the Survivor Man Scout badge out now,

474
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:14,000
which incorporates the sort of advanced survival techniques

475
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:15,000
that the boys can do.

476
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:17,000
And when they do certain ones,

477
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:19,000
they get the Survivor Man Scout badge,

478
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,000
which was an absolute thrill for me.

479
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:24,000
We're still working on, I guess, on the official launch of it,

480
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:27,000
but it's out and in operation.

481
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:29,000
And I'm going to keep doing stuff.

482
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:30,000
I've got more things on the go.

483
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
Will to Live is out now.

484
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:33,000
And my original book,

485
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:36,000
Survive Essential Skills and Tactics, the manual.

486
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:39,000
And I'm not going to stop.

487
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:41,000
I'm going to keep out and keep adventuring,

488
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:44,000
and I hope all the Scouts certainly do that as well.

489
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
I can't tell you how many Eagle Scout letters

490
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:49,000
I've signed for a lot of the boys in the States these days.

491
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:52,000
I've signed hundreds of them, which I'm thrilled to do.

492
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
So, yeah, we're going to keep at it.

493
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:55,000
Thank you so much, Les.

494
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,000
Thanks for taking the time again.

495
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:58,000
Not a problem.

496
00:27:58,000 --> 00:27:59,000
All right, thanks.

497
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:00,000
Okay, bye-bye.

498
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:01,000
Bye.

499
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:24,000
MUSIC

500
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:28,000
Tingle-ling, tingle-ling goes the telephone

501
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:32,000
Tingle-ling, tingle-ling goes my heart

502
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
And now here's one of your phone calls.

503
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:38,000
You too can pick up the phone and give us a call.

504
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:43,000
Tingle-ling goes my heart again

505
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:45,000
Hi, Clark.

506
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:49,000
Bill McFarland, and I'm with Troop 8, Fitzfield, Mass.

507
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:54,000
My favorite camping trip probably was back when I was around 14,

508
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
went off to summer camp.

509
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:59,000
It rained every day we were there.

510
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:02,000
And our campsite wasn't set up.

511
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
I was made a patrol leader.

512
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:07,000
We had to set up the campsite in the rain.

513
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:10,000
We were wet and muddy just about every day we were there.

514
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:13,000
And that definitely felt like a lot of fun,

515
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:17,000
but it was JLITC training.

516
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:20,000
And I had more fun with people that I didn't know,

517
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:24,000
but by the end of the week just became brothers

518
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:27,000
and that I wouldn't trade that experience for anything in the world.

519
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:30,000
Love the podcast. Keep up the good work.

520
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:33,000
Well, Bill, thanks for calling in with that memory.

521
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,000
Goes to show, doesn't it?

522
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:38,000
I mean, there's a couple of immutable laws of camping.

523
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:44,000
First one is that the rainiest, muddiest, most miserable camping trip

524
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:47,000
is the one that you are always going to remember the most fondly.

525
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:50,000
I don't know why that is, but that's the way it is.

526
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:55,000
The other law, it doesn't really matter where you go.

527
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:58,000
It's important who you go with.

528
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:01,000
I've had that proven again and again.

529
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:05,000
You can go to some pretty exciting places, anywhere on the globe,

530
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:10,000
exciting, challenging, wonderful, beautiful, world-class places.

531
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:16,000
And it's more important about who you go with.

532
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:19,000
It really is. That's what really makes the memories.

533
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:23,000
And that was a great memory, Bill. Really appreciate it.

534
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:26,000
You can call in with your favorite camping trip

535
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:29,000
or your question for the Scoutmaster podcast.

536
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:32,000
And you can actually be on the podcast. How about that?

537
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:35,000
And you're going to learn how to do that in just a minute.

538
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:54,000
Thanks for listening to this edition of the Scoutmaster podcast.

539
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:59,000
If you go to the blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com,

540
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:03,000
you'll see a bunch of links in the post that contains this podcast

541
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:07,000
to Les Stroud's new book, Will to Live, and his website,

542
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:11,000
and also the Survivorman Challenge that he mentioned during the interview.

543
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:15,000
I really appreciate Les being able to take the time to talk to us.

544
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:20,000
It was really exciting to be able to talk to somebody

545
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:23,000
I so admire for his work. Pretty amazing guy.

546
00:31:29,000 --> 00:31:31,000
Here's how to keep in touch.

547
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:37,000
Like I said, you can go to the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

548
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:40,000
And there you can do things like subscribe to the Scoutmaster newsletter,

549
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:44,000
which is a monthly digest of the Scoutmaster blog and podcast,

550
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:47,000
with some great scouting resources around a monthly theme.

551
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:52,000
And every newsletter includes a giveaway from one of our affiliate sponsors.

552
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:56,000
And next week I'll be announcing the winner of the February giveaway.

553
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:59,000
Subscribe to the podcast on iTunes.

554
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:04,000
And when you do, feel free to leave a comment or a review or a rating.

555
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:09,000
You can also call in to leave a voicemail comment or question.

556
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:13,000
That's right. You can call in and be part of the Scoutmaster podcast.

557
00:32:13,000 --> 00:32:19,000
Call 484-734-0002.

558
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:26,000
You can follow us at Scoutmaster blog on Facebook and ScoutmasterCG on Twitter.

559
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:30,000
Finally, you can email me, Clark Green, with your comments and questions

560
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:37,000
at ClarkGreen, C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N at gmail.com.

561
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:53,000
The Scoutmaster blog and the Scoutmaster podcast are not official publications

562
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:55,000
of the Boy Scouts of America.

563
00:32:55,000 --> 00:32:58,000
Nor are they endorsed or sponsored by the Boy Scouts of America.

564
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:02,000
Hey, hey, it's just me talking into a microphone,

565
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:06,000
tying the lend a hand to scout leaders and maybe have a little bit of fun along the way.

566
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:19,000
Speaking of fun, as I announced a couple times on the previous podcast,

567
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:25,000
I was part of a virtual training experience down in Murfreesboro, Tennessee

568
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:27,000
at Middle Tennessee University.

569
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:31,000
And just a shout out to the folks down there, and thanks Bob Martin

570
00:33:31,000 --> 00:33:33,000
for setting all of that up.

571
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:35,000
We had a good time. It was a lot of fun.

572
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:40,000
And we hope to do similar events soon, so keep an ear open.

573
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:44,000
Well, before we leave you, we want to hear from the founder of scouting,

574
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:46,000
Sir Robert Baden-Powell.

575
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:48,000
Sir Robert?

576
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:50,000
Good luck to you and good campings.

577
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:52,000
Why, thank you, sir.

578
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:54,000
Until next time.

579
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,000
Thank you.

